Enhancement: Track pack placement per backend in SlateDB metadata

SlateDB metadata previously recorded only a pack-level storage tier, which could
not describe repositories with several backends or different retention rules per
copy.

Vaultic now records each pack placement separately in the SlateDB metadata. The
repository config can declare placement backends with roles, offsite status,
failure domains, capacity and cost hints, minimum retention, retrieval class,
bandwidth ceiling, and object overhead. Existing single-backend and hot/cold
repositories resolve to the same behavior when no explicit placement registry is
configured.

`vaultic index check` now validates placement records, backend reverse indexes,
derived tier summaries, and the durability predicate. `vaultic index
rebuild-pack-stats` migrates known Phase 9 tier summaries into initial placement
records, rebuilds the derived tier summary, rebuilds pack and tier aggregates,
and rebuilds the backend reverse index.

`vaultic index stats` and `vaultic index packs` now use schema version 2 JSON and
can filter or group by backend and storage class. `index packs` also supports
`--not-offsite`, `--promotion-due`, and sorting by `offsite-deadline`.

SlateDB GC records placement eviction state and per-backend delete deadlines, so
a placement whose backend still enforces minimum retention can remain queued
while eligible placements are handled by later runs. Repack reads are warmed
through the existing warm-up path before reading archival packs, and constrained
placements use the configured cost model to decide whether repacking is worth
performing.

